{"Here is a weird game won by D. G. Mackay, of Balham, an irrepressible
character who could seriously claim to be the strongest player never to take
part in the British Championship. He seems to alternate between the Civil
Service and professional boxing, finding the latter both more enjoyable and
more lucrative. Every now and again he turns up at Bognor where his past
victims include O’Kelly de Galway, the Belgian who has won the event more
often than anybody else."} 1. c4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 e4 4. Ng5 f5 5. h4 Be7 6.
Nc3 h6 7. Nh3 Bxh4 8. e3 g6 9. Nf4 Kf7 10. c5 Bf6 11. Bc4+ Kg7 12. Nxg6 Rh7 13.
Nf4 Kh8 14. cxd6 Qxd6 15. Qh5 c6 16. Qg6 Rg7 17. Rxh6+ {Source: Illustrated
London News, 22 April 1961 (BH Wood column); CHESS, Vol.26, no.383-4, April
1961, p251} 1-0

